as "light" model longswords (and other fencing swords) have come into production and more popularity, there has been much discussion on the pros and cons of using lighter, more flexible swords for historical fencing. Detractors bemoan the inability to engage in binding and beating actions the same way, "sportification", and historical inaccuracies, while supporters cite lower injury risks and increased accessibility. it should be obvious to readers which side of this debate I land on personally, though my thoughts are somewhat nuanced: I recognize the downsides and drawbacks of lighter and more flexible swords, especially when taken to the extreme as in this project, but I see these ultralight longswords not as something to replace standard fencing longswords, but a supplemental tool for training. in the same sense, I see broader, heavier, "blunt" fencing longswords (such as the SIGI king) as a useful training tool as well, both the heavier broad bladed "realistic" swords and the ultralight swords providing utility it training different parts of longsword fencing (especially in the context of recreating a historical fencing practice, as opposed to just training for the purposes of competitive success, though I believe both have utility there too). 

with that said, some of the specific goals I had with this project were:

-experiment and have fun building something 
-create an extremely light, extremely flexible longsword trainer that can be used at high speed and intensity without creating as much risk for injury (especially concussion) and bruising as standard fencing longswords
-make it easy and simple to built and reproduce 

thus far in my testing and use of them, I have found the following benefits:
-allows lower* gear


the gear I have been using for these varies, from literally nothing (exclusively at a very controlled speed and intensity with my fiancee) to full standard longsword kit (at high speed and intensity sparring with my club). Generally, the level of gear I have been gravitating towards has been:
-mask with back of head
-gorget (sometimes)
-light jacket (AF coaches jacket)
-elbow guards
-light gloves with finger and wrist padding and hard knuckles
-light fencing pants
-knee guards

with this level of gear I consider myself mostly safe from injury, though 5-finer gloves with hard finger protection (such as thokks, high guards, black princes, or other medium saber/sidesword level gloves) would make me feel much more comfortable and at much lower risk for injury. with unpadded pants and jacket, you can definitely feel a sting on some cuts, and they may bruise. knee and elbow guards are probably not *strictly* necessary for injury prevention, but a hard cut with DEFINITELY hurt without at least some padding in those areas. one of the scarier injury risks with these is punctures, as they use epee blades, which are known to break much more easily and frequently than fencing longsword blades. while many are averse to blades breaking simply for the time and cost of replacement, the modularity of these, as well as the very low cost of epee blades (the blades I got were 24 USD each) means blade breakage is mostly only a concern for safety. given the issues with "rating" the puncture resistance of HEMA jackets, it would be wise to use a CE level 1 or 2 (350n or 800n) modern olympic fencing jacket, ideally in combination with a plastron (or underarm protector, not a hard chest protector (though that's a good idea for comfort too), but the puncture resistant half t-shirt that modern olympic fencing uses). these are also available readily and cheaply from modern olympic fencing manufacturers and retailers.

